   "body": "**Adversarial review (Opus 5.5, cloud/r1265-opus, round 1): REQUEST CHANGES** — head f94f35bf\n\nThe behaviour is correct. Every button gets the question it was shown for: `choose(_:for:)` takes the value from `presenting:`, and accept, link choice and decline read `current`, set `pending = nil` themselves and take `usedChangeCount` from `current`. The `.linkChoice` mapping is the same as before: `.acceptAttribute` gives `attribute: true`, and `.accept` goes through `acceptPending` to `attribute: false`. Esc, a tap outside and « Annuler » all end in `dismissPending` and put focus back on the text; running both paths does no harm. One test is missing, and some docs are stale.\n\n1. **Medium, `App/SwiftHFRTests/Composer/ComposerClipboardChoiceTests.swift:40-82`.** No test runs `choose` on a `.linkChoice` or an accepted `.formatting`. `eachQuestionNamesItsActions` only checks titles, and `ComposerFormattingModelTests` call `acceptLinkChoice` directly. Two mutants stay green:\n   - `.acceptAttribute: acceptLinkChoice(current, attribute: false)` in `ComposerClipboardChoice.swift:48`;\n   - swapping the kinds of the two `.linkChoice` entries at `:31-34`.\n   In both cases « Insérer [url=XXX] » silently inserts `[url]clip[/url]`, which breaks L01. **Fix:** with an empty selection, choose each `.linkChoice` button by its title and expect `[url=<clip>][/url]` and `[url]<clip>[/url]`. Add a `.formatting` « Insérer » case.\n2. **Low, stale docs and comments that still describe the old alert.**\n   - `docs/lots/LOT-174-formatting-toolbar.md:26` says \"the alert offers both legacy forms\" and still names « Sans le presse-papiers ».\n   - `ComposerFormattingModel.swift:31,48,66` say \"The alert's message\" and \"clipboard alert\".\n   - `docs/lots/LOT-085-composer-toolbar.md:44` says \"clipboard alerts\".\n   **Fix:** rename these to \"question\" / « Insérer sans le presse-papiers ».\n3. **Low, `ReplyComposerView.swift:108`.** The dialog is attached to the whole composer, not to the toolbar button. On iPad, and on iOS 26+ on iPhone, it shows as a popover pinned to the whole composer, not to the tapped button. « Annuler » is hidden there; a tap outside still cancels through the binding setter. The close dialog at `:115` does the same. **Fix:** put the Mac check from the issue on #893 (Esc on a hardware keyboard inserts nothing; where the popover appears on iPad).\n4. **Detail, PR body.** \"Red before `Pending.choices` existed\" is a compile failure, not a test that fails for the right reason. The test that really shows the change is the cancel test (the old view sent cancel to `declinePending`). Say so.\n\n**Verified**\n- Read the diff, the issue and its comments, and legacy `AnswerView.swift:530-557` @ 87fe0d1c. Read the model at the head.\n- `swiftlint lint --strict` on the 4 Swift files: clean.\n- `ledger.py check-body --pr 1265`: 2 blocks ok. The GAP-085 edit is the base text plus the #1180 clause.\n- #1223 only touches `ReplyComposerView.swift` at `@@ -148`, so it does not overlap.\n- Read with compiler rigour: `ForEach` in the dialog's actions, `Button(String, role:)`, and `Kind` getting `Hashable` without declaring it. No defect found.\n- Not built: Compile check was still queued, App build marked failure (draft gate).\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code)_",
